def content():
 item('precursors','compound-identities','Compound nomenclature and surface connectivity','Compound 1 is QDOH: CdS nanoclusters capped by sulfur-bound 4-hydroxythiophenolate. Compounds 2a–e retain that cap and esterify its phenolic oxygen with acetyl, butanoyl, pyrene-1-carbonyl, benzoyl and decanoyl groups, respectively. Compounds 3a–e are the corresponding N-acylimidazole reagents. The unnumbered drawing is a schematic surface representation, not an atom-resolved structure or a counted ligand shell.',2,'Unnumbered compound 1/2 illustration',records=('qdoh',*[f'ester-2{x}' for x in 'abcde']),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'),kind='reported_identity_and_schematic')
 item('precursors','reagent-grades','Reagent identity, grades and pretreatment','Aldrich reagent-grade sodium sulfide nonahydrate, imidazole, 1-pyrenecarboxylic acid, n-butanoyl chloride, decanoyl chloride and thionyl chloride are used as supplied. BDH reagent-grade acetic anhydride, butyric anhydride and benzoyl chloride are also used without further purification. n-Butanoyl chloride is listed as a purchased reagent, but the explicit 3b preparation uses butyric anhydride; do not silently make it a second complete route.',1,'Chemicals',records=('reagent-conditioning',))
 item('precursors','cadmium-ligand-conditioning','Cadmium acetate and capping ligand conditioning','Recrystallize reagent-grade cadmium acetate from glacial acetic acid and dry at 100 °C in vacuo. Distill reagent-grade 4-hydroxythiophenol under reduced pressure immediately before use. The source does not specify the cadmium-acetate hydration state, recrystallization solvent quantity, drying duration, distillation temperature or either pressure value.',1,'Chemicals',records=('reagent-conditioning','qdoh'))
 item('precursors','solvent-conditioning','Solvent preparation and grades','Dry reagent-grade toluene over 4 Å molecular sieves and filter. Use Aldrich ACS spectrophotometric DMSO without additional purification. Water passes through a Sybron-Barnstead D8902 mixed-bed ion-exchange column and then a D8204 activated-charcoal filter. Deuterated solvents from Cambridge Isotope Laboratories are supplied in sealed ampules and used immediately after opening. Drying time, sieve loading and gas purity are unreported.',1,'Chemicals',records=('reagent-conditioning',))
 item('precursors','qdoh-feeds','QDOH feed composition','The sulfide/ligand feed contains 2.53 g sodium sulfide nonahydrate (reported as 11 mmol) and 4.93 g 4-hydroxythiophenol (39 mmol) in 150 mL of acetonitrile/methanol/water, 1:1:2 by volume. The receiving solution contains 5.05 g cadmium acetate (reported as 29 mmol) in the same solvent system, but its volume is absent. Therefore 150 mL is not the total batch solvent volume. Retain the reported masses and amounts; the cadmium-acetate pair is inconsistent with the named chemical and hydration state is not specified.',1,'Preparation of QDOH',records=('qdoh',),compounds=('1',))
 item('precursors','pyrene-acid-feed','Pyrenecarbonyl chloride precursor','The upstream pyrene branch uses 1.0 g 1-pyrenecarboxylic acid (4.1 mmol) dissolved in 20 mL thionyl chloride under dry nitrogen. Thionyl chloride serves as reagent and reaction medium. The isolated acid chloride is used immediately for 3c; no isolated mass or quantitative yield is provided.',2,'1-Pyrenecarboxylic acid chloride',records=('pyrenecarbonyl-chloride','acylimidazole-3c'))
 item('precursors','acylimidazole-feeds','N-Acylimidazole feed branches','For 3a and 3b, imidazole is dissolved in a large excess of the corresponding acid anhydride; absolute quantities are absent. The other acylimidazoles use a 50% solution of the corresponding acyl chloride in dry toluene, added to a toluene solution containing 2.05 equivalents imidazole. The percentage basis, solution volumes, concentrations and feed rate are not supplied. The discussion rounds the imidazole requirement to two equivalents.',2,'N-Acylimidazoles',records=tuple(f'acylimidazole-3{x}' for x in 'abcde'),compounds=tuple(f'3{x}' for x in 'abcde'),extra=((4,'Acylimidazole preparation rationale'),))
 item('precursors','esterification-stocks','Representative esterification stocks','Only the acetyl example quantifies the two stocks: 300 mg QDOH sonicated in 5 mL DMSO, and 5 mL of solution containing 170 mg N-acetylimidazole in DMSO. The latter is a solution volume, not a separately stated solvent volume. These are capped-cluster powder and organic-reagent masses, not bare-CdS stoichiometric quantities or a known surface-OH molarity. Other derivatives share the described framework but have no independently stated reagent loads.',2,'Esterification',records=('ester-2a',),compounds=('1','2a','3a'))
 item('precursors','analytical-materials','Characterization and workup materials','Water, methanol, acetone and ether are used in derivative workup; QDOH workup omits methanol. KBr is the IR pellet matrix. QDOH and the esters use DMSO-d6 for NMR except CDCl3 for 2e; Figure 2 separately establishes DMSO-d6 for precursor 3e, while solvents for 3a–d are not individually given. D2O probes OH exchange. Chloroform is used for ester absorption/TEM suspensions, methanol for QDOH absorption. Carbon-coated copper grids, filter paper and a silicon calibration grid support TEM. Purities and quantities are stated only where the source supplies them.',2,'Workup, Spectroscopy and Electron Microscopy',records=('nmr','ftir','uv-visible','tem'),extra=((4,'Figure 2'),))
 item('protocol','qdoh-mixing','Nitrogen-protected kinetic trapping','Add the prepared sulfide/4-hydroxythiophenol feed to the rapidly stirring cadmium-acetate solution under nitrogen. Protect from light and continue stirring under nitrogen for 12 h; a bright yellow precipitate forms. The reaction temperature, addition duration, stirrer speed and nitrogen pressure are not given. Do not fill those conditions from the room-temperature drying or esterification procedures.',1,'Preparation of QDOH',records=('qdoh',),compounds=('1',))
 item('protocol','qdoh-isolation','QDOH concentration, washing and drying','Concentrate the reaction mixture to about one-third of its original volume using a rotary evaporator, then isolate the solid by centrifugation. Purify it by repeated washing, sonication and centrifugation, sequentially with water, acetone and ether. Dry the retained solid in high vacuum overnight at room temperature. The recovered yellow powder mass is 2.23 g; centrifugation settings, wash volumes/cycle counts and the numerical vacuum are absent.',2,'QDOH preparation continuation',records=('qdoh',),compounds=('1',))
 item('protocol','qdoh-dispersion','Dispersion check and operational solubility','Sonicating QDOH powder in DMSO gives a clear suspension without visible opalescence under room light, yet the He–Ne laser beam gives a pronounced Tyndall effect. Source note 10 defines solubility here as a transparent, nonsettling suspension that conventional centrifugation does not separate. This is not proof of molecular dissolution.',2,'QDOH preparation continuation',records=('qdoh','characterization'),compounds=('1',),extra=((3,'Reference/Note 10'),))
 item('protocol','pyrene-chloride-preparation','Preparation of 1-pyrenecarbonyl chloride','Stir the acid/thionyl-chloride solution at room temperature for several minutes under dry nitrogen; an olive-green precipitate forms. Heat to 80 °C for 3 h, during which it redissolves, then cool and filter. Remove excess thionyl chloride completely from the filtrate in vacuo. The fluorescent orange-yellow acid-chloride solid is immediately used for 3c. Neither cooling endpoint nor vacuum level is specified.',2,'1-Pyrenecarboxylic acid chloride',records=('pyrenecarbonyl-chloride',))
 item('protocol','anhydride-acylimidazoles','Anhydride route to 3a and 3b','Stir imidazole in excess acetic or butyric anhydride under dry nitrogen at room temperature, remove the liquid acid/excess anhydride under reduced pressure and recrystallize the resulting white crystals from toluene. The general method says 30 min for both reagents; Table 1 gives 30 min for 3a but 15 min for 3b. These reports remain separate rather than selecting a corrected time.',2,'N-Acylimidazoles',records=('acylimidazole-3a','acylimidazole-3b'),compounds=('3a','3b'),extra=((3,'Table 1'),))
 item('protocol','chloride-acylimidazoles','Acyl-chloride route to 3c, 3d and 3e','Add the 50% acyl-chloride/toluene solution dropwise with rapid stirring to the imidazole/toluene solution at room temperature. Heat to 100 °C and hot-filter the white imidazole-hydrochloride precipitate. Cool the filtrate in an ice bath to crystallize the N-acylimidazole, then recrystallize once from toluene. Table 1 reports 30, 15 and 30 min for 3c, 3d and 3e, but does not assign those times individually to addition, heating or crystallization.',2,'N-Acylimidazoles',records=('acylimidazole-3c','acylimidazole-3d','acylimidazole-3e'),compounds=('3c','3d','3e'),extra=((3,'Table 1 and Scheme 1'),))
 item('protocol','esterification-addition','Surface esterification at room temperature','Add the N-acetylimidazole/DMSO stock dropwise to the sonicated QDOH/DMSO suspension with rapid stirring, in the dark under dry nitrogen. Stir for 30 min at room temperature. This is the quantified example for 2a. The authors state similar conditions for all derivatives, and Table 1 gives DMSO/30 min for each of 2a–e; it does not report five complete batch formulations. The discussion describes usual completion within 15–30 min as a general observation, not a set of timed samples.',2,'Esterification',records=tuple(f'ester-2{x}' for x in 'abcde'),compounds=tuple(f'2{x}' for x in 'abcde'),extra=((3,'Table 1'),(4,'Acyl-group transfer discussion')))
 item('protocol','esterification-workup','Ice-bath quench and derivative isolation','Cool the reaction mixture in an ice bath and quench with water. Isolate the resulting precipitate by centrifugation; sonicate repeatedly and consecutively in water, methanol, acetone and ether. Dry in high vacuum at room temperature for 24 h. The water-quench volume, wash volumes, cycle counts, centrifugation settings and pressure are unreported. Keep this derivative workup separate from QDOH’s overnight drying sequence.',2,'Esterification',records=tuple(f'ester-2{x}' for x in 'abcde'),compounds=tuple(f'2{x}' for x in 'abcde'))
 item('protocol','unfunctionalized-control','Control without surface phenol','Unfunctionalized thiophenol-capped CdS clusters showed no reaction with N-acylimidazoles. Aqueous workup gave unchanged nanocluster and recovered organic acid. The authors use this to argue against simple chemisorption of reagent to the CdS core. Control amounts, selected acyl reagent, exposure time and a full upstream preparation are not provided.',5,'NMR/conversion discussion',records=('unfunctionalized-control',),kind='reported_control_and_author_interpretation')
 item('protocol','direct-acylchloride-failure','Initial direct-acyl-chloride attempts','The authors report that initial attempts to esterify with acyl chlorides destroyed the cluster. No reagent-specific amounts, temperature, duration or characterization of that trial are supplied. The neighboring statement that Fischer–Speier acidic conditions would destroy the cluster is a rationale, not a separately documented experiment.',3,'Results continuation before Scheme 1',records=('direct-acylchloride-degradation',),kind='qualitative_reported_failure_and_rationale',extra=((2,'Cluster lability discussion'),))
 item('protocol','prolonged-exposure','Prolonged exposure and cluster degradation','Extending reaction time did not improve the degree of esterification. Exposure of QDOH to the reaction conditions for more than 12 h destroyed the cluster, precipitated bulk CdS and yielded a crystalline organic product identified as the corresponding O,S-diester. This is a qualitative degradation observation with a lower time bound, not a calibrated failure probability or a fully specified bulk-CdS synthesis.',5,'IR/conversion discussion',records=('prolonged-degradation',),compounds=('1',),kind='qualitative_reported_degradation')
 item('structures','tem-preparation','TEM specimen preparation','Prepare a freshly sonicated and centrifuged suspension containing 2.5 mg nanocluster in 3 mL chloroform. Apply three drops to JBS-183 300-mesh carbon-coated copper grids, previously washed with acetone, chloroform and acetone and air-dried. Filter paper draws solvent through the grid, leaving clusters and aggregates; air-dry at room temperature. The source does not specify sonication time, centrifugation settings, drop volume or drying time.',2,'Electron Microscopy',records=('tem',))
 item('structures','tem-acquisition','TEM acquisition and measurement precision','TEM uses a Philips EM-301 with a precision silicon grid of 21,600 lines cm−1 for size calibration. Figure 6 reports 290,000× magnification and shows a 6 nm scale bar. Beam voltage, counted-particle totals and raw histograms are absent. Source note 15 states that the quoted precision is limited by microscope resolution.',2,'Electron Microscopy',records=('tem','characterization'),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'),extra=((6,'Figure 6 and Note 15'),))
 item('structures','tem-diameter-series','Measured particle dimensions','Table 3 reports TEM diameters of 30.4, 27.3, 24.7, 26.3, 28.2 and 28.2 Å for 1, 2a, 2b, 2c, 2d and 2e. The prose adds approximately 30.4 ± 7 Å for QDOH; the superscript 15 after 7 is a reference to the resolution caveat, not part of the uncertainty. No equivalent uncertainty is supplied for every derivative.',3,'Table 3',records=('characterization','tem'),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'),extra=((6,'TEM results and Note 15'),))
 item('structures','aggregation','Individual clusters and aggregates','The authors describe individual nanoclusters together with larger spheroidal aggregates for QDOH and its ester derivatives. The body assigns 1100 Å to a large aggregate associated with Figure 6, whose image bears a 6 nm scale bar. Preserve the text and image separately without measuring or silently correcting the aggregate size. Figure 6 identifies a dried QDOH/chloroform suspension even though the solubility discussion says QDOH is insoluble in chloroform; suspension and dissolution should not be conflated.',6,'TEM discussion, Figure 6 and solubility',records=('characterization','tem'),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'))
 item('structures','cap-gravimetry','Author-derived capping estimates','Assuming quantitative recovery of limiting sulfide and attributing excess mass to thiophenolate, the authors derive a cap contribution to sulfur. The preparation paragraph gives 20 mol%, whereas the discussion gives 34 mol%. Using specific gravity 4.8 and an approximately 30 Å spherical core, the discussion estimates 130 caps, area 2827 Å² and 22 Å² per cap. These are assumption-dependent estimates; neither fraction is a direct elemental measurement or a resolved ligand count.',2,'QDOH yield interpretation',records=('characterization',),compounds=('1',),kind='author_derived_model_with_source_conflict',extra=((3,'Gravimetric interpretation'),(4,'Coverage estimate continuation')))
 item('structures','prior-coverage-comparison','Prior capping-density comparisons','Applying the same model to earlier elemental analyses gives 25 Å² per cap for unfunctionalized CdS from reference 7 and 22 Å² per cap for pyridyl-functionalized CdS from reference 6. The authors compare these with their gravimetric QDOH estimate. These earlier-system values do not establish measured elemental composition or surface coverage for the current QDOH preparation.',4,'Capping-density comparison',records=('characterization',),kind='cited_prior_comparison')
 item('structures','structural-limits','Crystal and ligand-structure limits','The inspected main article supplies TEM and spectroscopic evidence, but no XRD pattern, SAED pattern, refined lattice parameters, measured atom coordinates or downloadable crystal structure. The cap drawing expresses proposed sulfur anchoring and phenolic/ester functionality. A future reference CdS lattice or ligand-bearing particle illustration must remain a reference model, not a structure measured for these samples.',6,'TEM results and conclusion',records=('characterization',),kind='reviewed_source_limit',extra=((2,'Compound illustration'),))
 item('properties','nmr-acquisition','NMR acquisition and solvent scope','A Bruker ARX 400 MHz instrument records 1H NMR. DMSO-d6 is used for QDOH and the esters except decanoyl derivative 2e, measured in CDCl3. Figure 2 explicitly shows organic reagent 3e in DMSO-d6; Figure 3 shows ester 2e in CDCl3. Residual-solvent/reference signals in those figures must not become nanocluster peaks.',2,'Spectroscopy',records=('nmr','characterization'),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e','3e'),extra=((4,'Figures 1–3'),))
 item('properties','qdoh-nmr-exchange','Phenolic proton assignment by D2O exchange','QDOH has broad aromatic signals from 6.0 to 7.3 ppm and a broad OH signal from 8.6 to 9.1 ppm with integration ratio 4:1. The authors interpret the structured broad resonances as multiple proton environments. A drop of D2O removes the OH signal in Figure 1b. No non-solvent signal is reported upfield of 5 ppm; residual water may be adsorbed at the surface. The exchange experiment supports the OH assignment without determining the number of ligands per particle.',4,'QDOH NMR discussion and Figure 1',records=('nmr','characterization'),compounds=('1',),kind='reported_observation_and_author_assignment')
 item('properties','nmr-table','NMR shifts and organic yields','Table 1 preserves all 11 compound rows, including multiplicities, integrals and assignments. The 2a–e percentages 95, 97, 95, 100 and 100 are phenolic-group conversions from residual-OH/aromatic integrations, not isolated inorganic yields. The 3a–e values 100, 50, 65, 100 and 100 are organic precursor yields. QDOH has no percentage in the table. The table’s 3d methyl assignment and its imidazole C4/C5 labels conflict with molecular identity or generalized discussion and remain unrepaired.',3,'Table 1',records=('characterization','nmr',*[f'acylimidazole-3{x}' for x in 'abcde']),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e','3a','3b','3c','3d','3e'),extra=((5,'NMR assignments and conversion determination'),))
 item('properties','decanoyl-nmr','Decanoyl precursor versus ester NMR','Figure 2 is 3e, the organic N-decanoylimidazole reagent, with DMSO residual marked by an asterisk. Figure 3 is nanocluster ester 2e, with chloroform, water and TMS marked separately. The ester aromatic region is broadened and structured; residual OH in some ester samples indicates incomplete conversion. These two spectra do not represent the same material state.',4,'Figures 2 and 3',records=('characterization','nmr','acylimidazole-3e','ester-2e'),compounds=('3e','2e'),extra=((5,'NMR interpretation'),))
 item('properties','ftir-acquisition','FTIR acquisition and complete band inventory','IR spectra were collected as KBr pellets using a Mattson 3000 Fourier-transform spectrometer. Table 2 gives all band positions, qualitative intensities, shapes and assignments for QDOH and 2a–e. Its legend distinguishes very strong, strong, medium, weak and very weak intensity; broad, very broad, sharp and shoulder features. Figure 4 is the decanoyl derivative 2e and displays percentage transmittance, despite being described as an absorption spectrum.',2,'Spectroscopy',records=('ftir','characterization'),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'),extra=((3,'Table 2'),(5,'Figure 4')))
 item('properties','qdoh-ir','IR evidence for phenolic sulfur-bound capping','QDOH’s para-substituted aromatic pattern includes a ring C=C band at 1488 cm−1 and strong out-of-plane C–H absorption at 819 cm−1. The OH stretch is 3301 cm−1 in Table 2/body; the preparation reports a strong broad 3302 cm−1 OH band and weak ring overtones at 1870 and 1734 cm−1. Table 2 instead lists the overtones at 1870 and 1730 cm−1. No phenyl S–H stretch is observed near 2580 cm−1, supporting sulfur-bound thiolate. The 1013 cm−1 OH-deformation prose assignment and Table 2’s 1009 cm−1 phenyl–S entry retain their separate locators.',4,'QDOH IR interpretation',records=('ftir','characterization'),compounds=('1',),kind='reported_spectrum_and_author_assignment',extra=((2,'QDOH preparation summary'),(3,'Table 2')))
 item('properties','prior-ir-comparison','Unfunctionalized-cap IR comparison','The unfunctionalized thiophenol-capped CdS comparator is described as having strong, sharp 686 and 734 cm−1 monosubstituted-phenyl absorptions and a weak four-band overtone pattern from 1730 to 1950 cm−1. This contrast supports the authors’ para-hydroxy-cap assignment. Those bands belong to the comparator and must not be added to the QDOH or ester peak lists.',4,'QDOH versus unfunctionalized-cap IR',records=('characterization',),kind='cited_prior_comparison')
 item('properties','ester-ir','IR changes after surface esterification','Esterification introduces strong carbonyl absorption, usually around 1750 cm−1, and reduces but may not eliminate the OH band near 3300 cm−1. Table 2 carbonyl values are 1758, 1751, 1720, 1736 and 1754 cm−1 for 2a–e. Aromatic derivatives 2c and 2d are interpreted as having resonance-coupled carbonyl groups. A weak-to-medium feature near 1015 cm−1 and broad 1200–1270 cm−1 absorption are assigned to sulfur-coupled ring motion and phenyl–O stretching, respectively.',5,'IR interpretation',records=('ftir','characterization'),compounds=('2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'),kind='reported_spectrum_and_author_assignment',extra=((3,'Table 2'),))
 item('properties','absorption-acquisition','Electronic-absorption measurement','The Hewlett-Packard 8452 diode-array spectrophotometer uses 1 cm quartz cuvettes. Ester spectra are measured in chloroform and QDOH in methanol. Figure 5 labels the QDOH dispersion 10−4 M, but the concentration basis is not established as particle, formula-unit or surface-group molarity. No PL quantum yield, Raman spectrum or electrical transport measurement is reported.',2,'Spectroscopy',records=('uv-visible','characterization'),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'),extra=((5,'Figure 5'),))
 item('properties','qdoh-absorption-conflict','QDOH absorption features and reporting conflict','The preparation summary lists a 295 nm edge and 305 nm shoulder. Table 3 and the optical discussion instead assign the CdS band-to-band onset near 390 nm, a 3.19 eV gap and a 0.66 eV confinement shift relative to bulk CdS at 2.53 eV. Preserve both source reports; do not substitute a peak read from Figure 5 or treat 295 nm and 390 nm as interchangeable training labels.',2,'QDOH preparation summary',records=('characterization','uv-visible'),compounds=('1',),extra=((3,'Table 3'),(5,'Electronic absorption and Figure 5')))
 item('properties','optical-size-series','Optical features versus model-derived sizes','Table 3 separates band-to-band absorption wavelengths, tight-binding-derived diameters and TEM diameters. QDOH and 2a, 2b, 2d and 2e have listed absorption values 390, 400, 420, 350 and 420 nm, corresponding to calculated 24, 25, 27, 19 and 27 Å. The 2c feature at 356–420 nm belongs to strong pyrene pi–pi* transitions; no CdS optical diameter is assigned because those transitions mask it. The statement that diameters remain identical after esterification is an author interpretation, not literal equality of table values.',3,'Table 3',records=('characterization','uv-visible'),compounds=('1','2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'),extra=((6,'Optical-size discussion'),))
 item('properties','missing-optical-equation','Source model reference without a displayed equation','Table 3 says the calculated diameters use Eq 1 and its footnote cites tight-binding band theory, reference 13. No numbered Equation 1 is displayed in the six supplied pages. The cited papers are not independently inspected in this task. Retain the reported calculated values and reference context without inventing a sizing formula or converting them into measured atomic dimensions.',3,'Table 3 heading and footnote',records=('characterization',),kind='reviewed_source_limit',extra=((6,'Reference 13'),))
 item('properties','solubility-qdoh','QDOH solvent compatibility','QDOH is reported insoluble in water, ether and hexane, and colloidally soluble in DMSO and DMF. Unlike unfunctionalized counterparts, it is also soluble in methanol and ethanol. The authors attribute this to phenolic hydrogen bonding. The later discussion states that QDOH is not soluble in chloroform, which should remain visible beside the TEM suspension caption.',3,'QDOH solubility and Note 10',records=('characterization',),compounds=('1',),kind='qualitative_observation_and_author_interpretation',extra=((6,'Solubility discussion and Figure 6'),))
 item('properties','solubility-derivatives','Derivative-specific solvent compatibility','All esters 2a–e are reported soluble in chloroform and insoluble in lower alcohols. The decanoyl ester 2e is soluble in ether; butanoyl 2b and decanoyl 2e are soluble in acetone. Acetyl 2a and benzoyl 2d are insoluble in both ether and acetone. DMSO solubility appears to vanish for the long-chain decanoyl ester. The paper does not provide every solvent outcome for pyrene derivative 2c, numerical solubility, concentration, equilibration time or a quantitative stability window.',6,'Solubility discussion',records=('characterization',),compounds=('2a','2b','2c','2d','2e'),kind='qualitative_source_observation')
 item('intuition','surface-handle','Why introduce a phenolic surface?','The authors want chemically addressable nanocluster surfaces that could tether chromophores, luminophores, molecular wires or redox centers. Sulfur binds the cap to CdS while the para phenolic group offers a separate synthetic handle. The reported products demonstrate surface derivatization; working molecular electronic devices and measured wire-mediated transport remain prospective applications.',1,'Abstract and Introduction',records=('qdoh','characterization'),kind='author_motivation_and_outlook',extra=((6,'Conclusions'),))
 item('intuition','mild-acyl-transfer','Why N-acylimidazoles?','The paper describes Cd–thiolate clusters as sensitive to acid/base conditions, heat, oxidation and competing sulfur chemistry. It selects N-acylimidazoles, used for phenolic tyrosine tagging in reference 9, to transfer acyl groups under mild conditions. Successful short-time derivatization and failures under direct-acyl-chloride or prolonged treatment support a limited reaction window; they do not identify a complete kinetic mechanism.',2,'Cluster-lability discussion',records=('direct-acylchloride-degradation','prolonged-degradation',*[f'ester-2{x}' for x in 'abcde']),kind='author_chemical_rationale',extra=((3,'Scheme 1 and reference 9'),(5,'Degradation observations')))
 item('intuition','imidazole-stoichiometry','Role of the second imidazole equivalent','In the authors’ mechanism, the N-acylated cation is more acidic than protonated imidazole, allowing the second imidazole equivalent to capture HCl and precipitate as imidazole hydrochloride. This rationale explains the 2.05-equivalent chloride-route preparation. It is separate from using excess acid anhydride for 3a/3b and does not establish unreported stoichiometric quantities.',4,'Acylimidazole preparation rationale',records=tuple(f'acylimidazole-3{x}' for x in 'abcde'),kind='author_mechanistic_interpretation',extra=((2,'N-Acylimidazoles'),))
 item('intuition','integrity-evidence','What supports retained nanocluster integrity?','NMR/IR changes support ester formation, the unfunctionalized control argues against simple core adsorption, and TEM/optical size estimates remain in the nanocluster range. These observations support the authors’ retention-of-integrity argument within resolution and model limits. They do not demonstrate a defect-free crystal, atomically unchanged core, complete conversion or identical particles before and after reaction.',5,'Conversion, control and IR discussion',records=('unfunctionalized-control','characterization'),kind='author_interpretation_with_reviewed_limits',extra=((6,'Optical and TEM comparison'),))
 item('intuition','surface-solvent-affinity','How surface chemistry changes solvent affinity','Loss of phenolic OH accompanies loss of lower-alcohol compatibility, while longer aliphatic ester groups increase compatibility with acetone/ether. The authors use these trends to connect outer-surface functionality with dispersion behavior. They report qualitative solvent compatibility rather than a continuous chain-length/solubility law.',6,'Solubility discussion',records=('characterization',),kind='author_chemical_interpretation')
 item('intuition','future-questions','Evidence-guided questions for later work','Potential extensions include measuring surface conversion independently of broad NMR integrations, resolving cap stoichiometry, following colloidal stability quantitatively, and separating core-size changes from ligand/chromophore absorption. These are curator-proposed questions motivated by the paper’s reporting limits, not experiments or novelty claims made by this source.',3,'Tables 1–3',records=('characterization',),kind='curator_proposed_outlook',extra=((5,'Optical/IR interpretation'),(6,'Conclusions')))
 item('sources','scope-and-missing-si','Scope of the supplied-source review','All six supplied main pages, including tables, captions, compound illustration, scheme and references, are covered. Matching SI is not located or verified in the authorized local collections. Cited preparations and prior comparison data retain citation-only status unless this article itself reports the required facts. No missing conditions are filled from another paper.',1,'Main identity and local-source review',kind='review_scope',extra=((6,'Closing references'),))
 item('sources','missing-operational-fields','Unreported operational fields','The main text omits the cadmium-feed volume, QDOH synthesis temperature, precise gas/vacuum conditions, several stock loads, percentage-concentration basis, dropwise feed rate, wash volumes/cycles, centrifugation conditions and most sonication durations. Room temperature, overnight and several minutes retain qualitative wording. These gaps prevent treating the historical report as a fully specified operating procedure.',1,'QDOH preparation',records=('qdoh',*[f'ester-2{x}' for x in 'abcde']),kind='reviewed_source_missingness',extra=((2,'Preparation methods'),))
 item('sources','sample-and-data-limits','Sample identity, structures and raw-data limits','Compound labels distinguish 1, 2a–e and 3a–e; they do not identify independently replicated batches or prove that every spectrum and TEM image comes from the same aliquot. Original figures and tabulated data remain available, but no raw spectral arrays, complete size histograms or experimental atom coordinates are supplied. Characterization observations and organic-precursor yields are not additional CdS synthesis routes.',3,'Tables 1–3',records=('characterization',),kind='reviewed_source_missingness',extra=((4,'Figures 1–3'),(5,'Figures 4–5'),(6,'Figure 6')))
 # Explicit full table content is reachable as structured rows and individual
 # compound summaries. Numeric facts are source-reported, not plot digitization.
 for row in TABLE1:
  c=row['compound'];pct=row['yield_or_conversion_percent'];role=row['quantity_role']
  text=f"Table 1 reports {row['nmr']} (chemical shifts in ppm). "
  text+=(f"The reported percentage is {pct}%, classified as {role.lower()}." if pct is not None else 'No percentage yield is tabulated for this compound.')
  if row.get('conflict'):text+=' '+row['conflict']
  item('properties','nmr-row-'+c,'Table 1: compound '+c,text,3,'Table 1, row '+c,records=('characterization','nmr'),compounds=(c,))
 for row in TABLE2:item('properties','ir-row-'+row['compound'],'Table 2: compound '+row['compound'],'Reported IR bands in cm−1: '+row['bands_and_assignments']+'. Qualitative intensities and assignments are those of the source; no missing peak positions are read from the plot.',3,'Table 2, row '+row['compound'],records=('characterization','ftir'),compounds=(row['compound'],))
